\cf0 The 
\f1\b 57 Machiavellianism
\f0\b0  score is the interesting counterweight. It doesn't necessarily mean manipulation. At a moderate level, it can represent an ability to understand 
\f1\b power, incentives, agendas, organizational politics and strategic timing
\f0\b0 .\
That can actually be valuable: ethical leaders still need political awareness.\
The risk comes if strategic thinking starts replacing openness\'97for example, 
\f1\b \'93I know what is really going on here, so I'll manage the situation indirectly rather than have the difficult conversation.\'94
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Potential biases\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 A few possible biases are worth testing rather than assuming:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 1. \'93I am acting ethically, therefore my judgment is probably sound.\'94
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 High ethical standards can paradoxically create blind spots. You may be especially confident in decisions that feel principled to you.\

\f1\b 2. \'93I understand what people need.\'94
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 Your empathy score suggests good interpersonal awareness, but empathy is not mind-reading. You may occasionally infer someone's motives or feelings without checking.\

\f1\b 3. \'93I'm being strategic, not political.\'94
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 Your moderate Machiavellianism could make you relatively comfortable reading organizational dynamics. The danger is becoming overly suspicious of motives or communicating differently to different audiences.\

\f1\b 4. Humility becoming under-assertiveness.
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 An 82 humility score is a strength, but very high humility can sometimes turn into reluctance to claim expertise, negotiate firmly, challenge poor decisions, or advocate for yourself.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

